00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:04.320
Quick description of
why this slide is here.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:10.320
So, we all have been doing
security for a long time.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.890
And it doesn't-- we've been
doing it for a very long time,

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.590
ever since we started putting
computers on the internet,

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:19.330
but it's still a
really hard problem.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.410
And the problem is, as
our security techniques

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.205
evolve, attacks evolve.

00:06:24.205 --> 00:06:26.580
And then we're like, OK, it's
the chicken and egg problem

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:27.080
again.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.350
We're still chasing
and trying to fix this.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:31.200
So what do we do?

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.500
We can't build a one
size fits all solution,

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:37.140
every organization is
different, every company

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:40.000
has different needs, and
that's totally understandable.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.820
And the hard-shelled
perimeter model, or I

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.570
think it's also called
the eggshell model,

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.910
it doesn't work
very well anymore.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:50.340
Because we've seen examples
in the past where infiltrators

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:51.548
have been inside the company.

00:06:51.548 --> 00:06:53.880
And that's not going
to work anymore.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.980
So we take micro segmentation
at every layer very seriously.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:58.770
And that's what we
think about security

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:00.640
as a defense in-depth model.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:02.140
Also referred to
as the onion model,

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:04.350
which is why you see
that picture there.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:08.820
And to relate that
onion model to Anthos,

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.070
we really think about Anthos
in three different layers

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:12.120
of infrastructure.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:13.770
We think of them
starting at the bottom

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.960
of the pyramid with nodes.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:16.890
We all know our VMs.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:17.700
We love our VMs.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:18.830
They're awesome.

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:20.580
And we've also protected
them in firewalls

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:22.300
for an eternity at this point.

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:25.320
So we're not going to
talk about firewalls.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:31.270
And the next level up from
nodes is pods, Kubernetes pods,

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:34.295
which is somewhere in the
middle of nodes and services.

00:07:34.295 --> 00:07:36.420
And then finally, at the
top we have Istio services

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:40.350
which is our new service mesh
that we're talking about.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:46.320
So those are those three levels
that we're imagining Anthos as.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.330
And we want to
think about security

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:51.690
at each of these layers to
give you a very strong defense

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:52.800
in-depth security posture.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:57.460
It actually still
says that the clusters

00:07:57.460 --> 00:07:59.790
are in California, Illinois,
North Carolina, and Texas.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:03.040
So just imagine that you
had states mapped there.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.340
So, I want to motivate the
example here a little bit more.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:08.840
Because we're really talking
about a multi cluster now.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:10.430
We've done one cluster.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:14.200
We're now going out and
deploying multiple clusters

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:18.202
all around the country,
the globe, what have you.

00:08:18.202 --> 00:08:20.410
And what we want to try to
do is understand what sort

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:22.570
of problems we get into
when we start talking

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:24.680
about security at this scale.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:25.990
So imagine a retail bank.

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:28.900
I mean, this could
be retail anything.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:30.910
A retail bank, it's
running on Anthos.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:34.960
What that means is they've
deployed four unique GKE

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:36.700
on-prem clusters.

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:40.390
So they're all independent
standalone GKE clusters running

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:42.400
on premises in the branch.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:44.620
And the idea there
is that the bank

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:47.620
has realized that real time
services for their customers

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:48.770
is super important.

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:51.640
So they've taken the step and
they've adopted GKE on-prem--

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.260
that's great.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.582
And they've deployed
four for now.

00:08:55.582 --> 00:08:57.790
They've gone to California,
Illinois, North Carolina,

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.030
and Texas.

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:02.260
So this is great, but now you're
already asking the question,

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.660
how do I keep these
things in sync?

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:08.470
Because you can change one
thing on one cluster, and then

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:11.050
suddenly, all of your other
clusters are out of sync.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:11.980
That's not all.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:15.370
Once that starts happening,
you have fleet fragmentation,

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:19.180
because your app's start
diverging in versions.

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:21.100
And the worst part of
it all is that leads

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.890
to all your development teams
adopting a different workflow

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:25.270
for production and deployment.

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:27.985
And that is a security
nightmare for every one of us.

00:09:27.985 --> 00:09:29.110
So we want to prevent that.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:35.792
Just to motivate the
example a bit more,

00:09:35.792 --> 00:09:37.750
I want to show you what
they're running inside.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.228
It's a simplistic example.

00:09:39.228 --> 00:09:41.020
We've obviously curated
the details for you

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:43.340
to get the most out
of this session.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:45.240
It could be a much
more complex setup.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:49.060
But imagine each cluster
is running three services

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:53.920
primarily-- account summary,
products, and PII database.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:57.520
The idea here is that a teller
could come to the home page

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:01.900
and look up a customer's account
summary by making a request.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:04.850
And the account summary can
get access to PII database.

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:07.450
The product service, or any
other service, for that matter,

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:09.880
should not be able to
get to the PII database

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:11.630
under any circumstances.

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:14.082
So, that's our sort of
setup for this problem.

00:10:14.082 --> 00:10:15.790
And we're going to
work through this step

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:19.570
by step to see how we can
use all the layers of Anthos,

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:22.508
namely Kubernetes and Istio,
to protect this and get

00:10:22.508 --> 00:10:23.550
the posture that we want.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:28.582
OK, network policy--
how many of you

00:10:28.582 --> 00:10:29.790
have heard of network policy?

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:31.050
Kubernetes Network Policy?

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:36.230
OK, maybe like a fourth, or
maybe a third of the room,

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:37.490
maybe.

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:40.820
OK, so that's good.

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:41.870
Why do we need it?

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:44.980
So, we have pods, that I
told you about earlier.

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:49.340
Pods, when you deploy Kubernetes
pods today in your system,

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:52.100
by default they can
talk to anything.

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:53.810
This can be a good
thing and a bad thing,

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:54.950
depending on how
you're looking at it.

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:56.240
From a developer's
point of view,

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:57.320
it's awesome,
because you can get

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:58.400
access to whatever you want.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.025
From a security
person's point of view,

00:11:00.025 --> 00:11:01.882
it's not cool,
because you need to be

00:11:01.882 --> 00:11:04.340
able to lock it down and make
sure that the surface area is

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:05.330
protected.

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:08.870
So keeping that in mind, we have
this feature in Kubernetes--

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.220
it's OSS.

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:13.430
We fully support
it in GCP and GKE.

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:18.200
So you can think of it as
a firewall for your pods,

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:19.290
of sorts.

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:24.440
What it does is, it cuts down
any sort of restricted traffic

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:25.260
out of the system.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:28.010
So your pods can never
go out and do stuff

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:29.420
that you don't want them to do.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:32.910
So that's the idea behind this.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:38.460
You can express rich ingress and
egress rules using this APII.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:41.430
You can actually write IP
addresses and sider blocks

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:42.940
if you choose to.

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:44.820
I recommend not.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:46.110
You can do that though.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:48.308
You can use Kubernetes labels.

00:11:48.308 --> 00:11:50.100
And you can also use
Kubernetes namespaces.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:54.120
So that's awesome, because
it's a first class citizen.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:57.450
And finally, if you're already
using namespaces in Kubernetes

00:11:57.450 --> 00:12:00.750
to support your
workloads and keep

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:02.880
them grouped and
separated, in some sense,

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:05.520
logically, you can use
Kubernetes Network Policy

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:09.690
really easily, as it is
a namespace property.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:12.580
So it applies to all your pods
within the namespace instantly.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:13.830
So that's really, really cool.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:19.810
All right, so let's bring
it back to the example

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:21.020
that we looked at earlier.

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:23.740
We had our account summary
service, we had products.

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:26.480
And our mission was we
don't want any other service

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:28.420
except for account
summary service

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:31.310
to be able to talk
to the PII database.

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:33.430
So here, I've set
up a simple policy,

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:34.990
a network policy object.

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:38.218
It's missing a few details, but
the key things are highlighted.

00:12:38.218 --> 00:12:39.760
What we're trying
to do here is we're

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:43.110
trying to select the
PII database pods.

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:44.648
So I want to emphasize
the fact here

00:12:44.648 --> 00:12:45.940
that we're selecting pods here.

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:47.990
We're not talking about
services just yet.

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:49.430
We're talking about pods.

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:51.650
We're still in the middle
of the VMs and services.

00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:53.870
So we're at pods.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:56.470
So we're selecting
our PII database pods.

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:58.630
And what we're
doing is, the moment

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:01.150
we implement a network policy
selecting the PII database

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:04.720
pods, all the traffic is
cut from PII database.

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:07.480
So the PII database pods
cannot go out to the internet,

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:09.790
cannot go to other
services or other pods.

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:12.440
Other pods can't come
to the PII database.

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:14.110
It's a purely white list model.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:17.590
So a white list model means
you have to explicitly now tell

00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:21.250
the system what traffic is
allowed to come to PII database

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:23.800
or go from the PII database.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:26.500
So the words we use for that
is ingress and egress, which

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:28.760
I'm sure all of you
are very familiar with.

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:33.370
So just to sort of summarize
that the comment, the moment

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:35.740
you create a network policy
object, and you select

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:40.930
all the PII database pods,
all the communication,

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:44.590
all network traffic, is cut
off from them immediately.

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:46.330
Now you go take the
next step, and you

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:49.570
say, OK, I want to allow
ingress from account summary

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:50.640
to PII database.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:52.390
So, you say, OK, well,
I'm going to select

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:55.160
all the pods that are running
accounts summary service.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.730
And I'm going to let them
talk to PII database.

00:13:57.730 --> 00:13:58.450
That's great.

00:13:58.450 --> 00:14:00.790
And that actually
solves our problem here.

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:04.450
So we've talked about ingress,
but I left out the egress part

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:05.170
just yet.

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:09.115
So you can actually either
mention the egress keyword here

00:14:09.115 --> 00:14:10.240
or leave it out completely.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:14.170
Because as I mentioned earlier,
by putting it under a policy,

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:16.010
you've already cut
traffic from it entirely.

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:17.350
So it's a white list model.

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:19.830
If you do not enable
explicitly any egress sources,

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:22.330
no traffic is leaving your pods
and you can be sure of that.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.100
All right, we're
moving a level up now.

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.440
So we've talked about
our pods, that's great.

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:34.460
We've secured them at a lower
level of the infrastructure,

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:35.270
that's fantastic.

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:36.395
We've cut our service area.

00:14:36.395 --> 00:14:38.300
We know for sure
that the IP traffic

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:41.100
we don't want to reach the
pods is not reaching them,

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:42.050
that's awesome.

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:44.510
But now, we need to start
thinking about our services.

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:49.880
So, who here has already heard
about Istio security policy.

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:51.980
Almost the same
fraction of people.

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:55.030
Not the same people, the
same fraction of people.

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:57.567
OK, so what we're
going to try to do

00:14:57.567 --> 00:14:59.900
is just-- actually, how many
of you have heard of Istio,

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:03.510
and understand what
Istio is almost?

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:05.140
OK, good.

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:06.500
I'll try to just--

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:07.750
I came up with a cool analogy.

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:08.542
I think it's cool--

00:15:08.542 --> 00:15:10.510
I'm biased-- I'm sorry.

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:14.110
I'll give you a cool analogy
to how I understand Istio.

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:18.010
So imagine you're a host at
a birthday party with lots

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:19.480
of children running around.

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:21.640
These children
are microservices.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:24.320
And you're trying to protect
them from getting hurt.

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:28.163
So if you can actually strap
balloons and stuff across

00:15:28.163 --> 00:15:30.080
everybody, that would
be great, but you can't.

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:31.060
There's too many
kids running around.

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:32.120
You can't do that.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:34.495
So you can imagine Istio coming
in and helping you there,

00:15:34.495 --> 00:15:36.328
and saying, OK, I'm
going to let you control

00:15:36.328 --> 00:15:38.470
these microservices, because
luckily microservices

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:42.420
are easier to control than
kids are at a birthday party.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:44.410
But yeah, that's my analogy.

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:47.440
Feel free to use
it if you want to.

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:48.920
That's where we're
going with this.

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:53.650
So to control all your
Istio microservices,

00:15:53.650 --> 00:15:57.190
you need to put security
policies in place.

00:15:57.190 --> 00:15:58.310
What do we have here?

00:15:58.310 --> 00:15:59.980
So we, first of all,
start with strongly

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:03.250
authenticating all our
services, as well as our users.

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:06.160
So Istio by itself
issues strong identities,

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:07.840
and strong non-mutable
identities,

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:11.540
to each service that's
running inside your mesh.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:15.610
So this is great, because we'll
talk about the next point here

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:16.180
in a moment.

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:18.430
But this is great because
it's non-mutable identities.

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:20.138
You don't have to lift
a finger for this.

00:16:20.138 --> 00:16:22.540
Istio does it for you
right off the bat.

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:24.790
And users are also
authenticated by the system,

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:26.350
but the identity is not issued.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:28.547
We'll touch on that
in more detail later.

00:16:28.547 --> 00:16:29.380
So let's keep going.

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:34.460
Encryption for everyone--
so, because we've

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:36.360
authenticated everybody
in the system,

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.360
including users,
including services,

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:41.240
we can use that identity
to encrypt and establish

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:44.250
an MTLS channel between all
your traffic within the mesh.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:46.910
So you can get service
to service encryption

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:49.260
for basically free, without
having to change anything.

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:53.380
And context based
authorization--

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:55.900
so we've done authentication,
we've done encryption.

00:16:55.900 --> 00:16:57.640
But we also want to
do authorization,

00:16:57.640 --> 00:17:00.370
because we want to make
sure that only certain parts

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:03.040
of the system are accessible
and certain parts are completely

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:04.000
off access.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:08.069
Because, really, access is
really a non-binary thing.

00:17:08.069 --> 00:17:09.510
It's more of in a spectrum.

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:11.260
Some people have access
to certain things,

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:12.829
some people have
access to other things.

00:17:12.829 --> 00:17:15.287
So we're going to look at how
to do partial access as well.

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:19.730
OK, going back to our example--

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:21.560
let's start with authentication.

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:24.140
In this case, I've set
up a simple example.

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:26.300
Again, these are some of
the most common examples I

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:27.690
see when people have questions.

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:29.390
So there's more details.

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:32.260
If you keep reading
more, and more, and more,

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:35.600
you'll get very excited
about security, as I do.

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:38.960
But here, I've taken a simple
example of user authentication.

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:43.982
What's happening here is
we're using a policy object,

00:17:43.982 --> 00:17:45.440
Istio policy object
here, and we're

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:47.360
selecting the PII
database service,

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:50.210
in this case-- not pods,
we've moved up a layer.

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:52.420
So this is the Istio--

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:54.320
the PII database is
the Istio service

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:55.730
that we're targeting here.

00:17:55.730 --> 00:17:58.070
And we're selecting it
under this policy object.

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:01.550
And we're saying
only let users who

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:05.840
have jot tokens issued by
Acme.com access this service.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:08.340
So jot tokens, if
people are familiar,

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:12.470
they're a common OIDC
protocol implementation.

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:16.240
And a lot of times it could
be Active Directory, or OCTA,

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:18.400
or any other provider like that.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:20.330
But Istios uses
jot the time being.

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:22.650
And in this case,
you could provide--

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:25.830
assign a token from
Acme.com, and the system

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:27.410
will let you through,
which is great.

00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:29.375
Because if it was
coming from Fakeme.com,

00:18:29.375 --> 00:18:30.500
it won't be allowed access.

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:35.840
Encryption in
transit-- so, we've

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:37.970
done identity, that's awesome.

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:41.460
How do we take it from there
to giving you encryption?

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:43.280
Well, you don't
have to do anything.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:44.570
The system does it for you.

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:45.860
How does it do it?

00:18:45.860 --> 00:18:49.310
We have a little thing called
Envoy, a sidecar proxy that's

00:18:49.310 --> 00:18:51.380
part of this Istio bundle.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:53.870
The Istio bundle
installs the Envoy proxy

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:56.270
in front of all of
your microservices.

00:18:56.270 --> 00:18:59.540
And every time
you have a service

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:01.220
that wants to talk
to another service,

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:02.640
if you enable this policy--

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:04.220
so in this case,
what we're doing is

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:07.370
the PII database service is
being targeted by this policy,

00:19:07.370 --> 00:19:09.980
and it's saying all my
peers should use MTLS

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:11.790
while talking to me.

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:14.360
The curly brackets there
not specifying a service

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:17.220
means all services.

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:20.367
So what this does is, it
forces all of the people

00:19:20.367 --> 00:19:21.950
or all of the services
that are trying

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:26.630
to talk to PII database
ensure that they MTLS enabled

00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:28.610
or encryption enabled.

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:30.575
The one thing I
didn't show here is--

00:19:30.575 --> 00:19:31.950
it's called the
destination rule.

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:34.040
Ray will briefly touch
upon that in his demo,

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:36.320
to show you how
account summary can

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:39.860
start talking to PII
database using MTLS

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:41.990
by setting a destination
rule after this policy.

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:49.940
Authorization-- so we've
done two things so far.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:52.610
We've prevented untrusted
access to the system

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:54.707
and we've prevented man
in the middle attacks.

00:19:54.707 --> 00:19:55.790
But that's not sufficient.

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:59.150
As I mentioned earlier,
a lot of times your users

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:00.830
have partial access
to the system.

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:03.600
They don't have full
access to the system.

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:05.240
So how do you go
about doing that?

00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:07.626
So, in our little
example here, I

00:20:07.626 --> 00:20:10.910
want to allow tellers to only be
able to read the PII database,

00:20:10.910 --> 00:20:13.333
but not write anything
to the database.

00:20:13.333 --> 00:20:14.750
So what we're going
to do here is,

00:20:14.750 --> 00:20:18.920
we're going to set up a
service roll call PII viewer.

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:21.050
And what that role
is doing here is,

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:24.140
it's granting get permissions
to the PII database.

00:20:24.140 --> 00:20:26.990
So the get would be the
HTTP equivalent of read.

00:20:26.990 --> 00:20:30.920
And when you associate
that service role

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:34.790
with a service role binding, and
in that service role binding,

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:37.190
what you're specifying
is that the access has

00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:39.200
to come from accounts
summary, and it

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:41.400
has to come from a
group called tellers.

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:43.610
So that group is actually
coming from the jot token

00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:46.140
that I mentioned earlier.

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:49.080
These are details you can read
about more if you wanted to

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:52.160
or I'm happy to answer questions
afterwards if you have any.

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:56.840
But the idea here is that
you're very clearly specifying

00:20:56.840 --> 00:20:59.990
in your authorization
policy what

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:02.090
is allowed to access
your PII database service

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:03.500
and what is not.

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:05.480
And only the tellers
group can do a read here.

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.120
So we've talked about
several policies already.

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:16.080
And I want to show a picture
of how they all fit together

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:20.597
and how they work in
harmony, so to speak.

00:21:20.597 --> 00:21:21.930
This is the path we really want.

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:24.270
We want a user at Acme.com
or a teller at Acme.com

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:26.490
to be able to access
account summary

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:29.940
and be able to get
to PII database.

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:33.990
So we lock down the access from
PII database to the internet,

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:36.420
and we lock down any other
service, any other pods,

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:38.670
from being able to
reach the PII database.

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:41.010
So we cut that out first.

00:21:41.010 --> 00:21:43.740
That was our first network
policy object that we created.

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:48.570
It locked down access from
products to PII database.

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:50.850
We also cut out
access to internet.

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:53.660
So PII database, if it had
any faulty code or anything

00:21:53.660 --> 00:21:55.720
and it inadvertently tried
to go to the internet,

00:21:55.720 --> 00:21:58.830
that's all cut out also.

00:21:58.830 --> 00:22:00.420
Then we went into
off authentication.

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:01.830
We cut out access
from Fakeme.com

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:03.720
and any other
websites that might

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:08.340
be issuing tokens and trying
to access your system.

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:10.830
We enabled encryption, so
that account summary and PII

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:13.113
database are always talking
on an encrypted channel.

00:22:13.113 --> 00:22:15.030
So that none of your man
in the middle attacks

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:16.405
can actually steal
data from you.

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:23.830
We also cut down the HTTP post
request from user at Acme.com.

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:26.290
What we're trying to
do here is very clearly

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:28.930
draw a line between what's
allowed from the tellers group

00:22:28.930 --> 00:22:30.920
and what's not.

00:22:30.920 --> 00:22:33.790
And if the teller got
really clever here,

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:35.980
and tried to bypass
the account summary,

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:37.670
that would also be rejected.

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:41.660
So the only path left in
the system is this one.

00:22:41.660 --> 00:22:43.120
And that's what we
just implemented

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:45.130
with three simple policies.

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:50.650
So I just want to just show you
the power of this sort of fly

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:53.740
by wire security system,
where you're doing everything

00:22:53.740 --> 00:22:54.970
based on policy.

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:57.070
You're not configuring
firewalls anymore.

00:22:57.070 --> 00:23:00.520
You're not configuring
manual switches anymore.

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:02.740
This is moving above a layer.

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:05.140
And it's giving you a real
fly by wire experience,

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:07.840
because you can do all this
by setting a few policies.

00:23:07.840 --> 00:23:09.940
And all of the fabric
will change according

00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:11.890
to your requirements,
to give you

00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:14.655
as high a micro segmentation
as you could possibly imagine.

00:23:17.390 --> 00:23:20.140
Just to quickly distinguish
between the two policies

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:21.140
that we've talked about.

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:23.015
We've talked about
Kubernetes Network Policy.

00:23:23.015 --> 00:23:25.610
We've talked about
Istio Security Policy.

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:28.460
You might be asking why
we have to use both.

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:31.640
The real answer is, they're
both good at really--

00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:34.280
they're really good
at different things.

00:23:34.280 --> 00:23:37.010
So Kubernetes Network Policy
is really good at cutting down

00:23:37.010 --> 00:23:40.940
your attack surface area, while
Istio Security Policy gives you

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:44.490
fine grained access control
at the service level.

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:46.482
So you need the umbrella
and the raincoat

00:23:46.482 --> 00:23:47.690
to protect you from the rain.

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:51.800
And now we have our demo.

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:52.850
Please welcome Ray.

00:23:52.850 --> 00:23:53.750
RAY COLLINE: So hi everyone.

00:23:53.750 --> 00:23:54.417
I'm Ray Colline.

00:23:54.417 --> 00:23:55.700
I'm a software engineer.

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:58.793
And I work on Anthos
Config Management.

00:23:58.793 --> 00:24:00.460
And today what we
want to do is take you

00:24:00.460 --> 00:24:03.550
through a demo, which is going
to show a lot of the concepts

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:07.310
that Gobind just talked
about in his presentation.

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:09.010
And if you've
installed Istio, it

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:11.140
comes with an
example application,

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:14.080
which looks a lot
like the bank example

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:17.570
that Gobind just
said, or talked about.

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:20.500
So here we have a ratings app.

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:22.960
You can think of this
as the PII database.

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:26.830
You have a reviews system,
which you can think

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:29.530
of as like the account summary.

00:24:29.530 --> 00:24:32.380
And then you have basically
a product page at the front.

00:24:32.380 --> 00:24:35.080
End these three things
then get requests.

00:24:35.080 --> 00:24:38.062
And then they're allowed to talk
to these different backends.

00:24:38.062 --> 00:24:40.270
I'm also going to be demoing
Anthos Config Management

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:43.450
as a way to deploy
the Bookinfo app

00:24:43.450 --> 00:24:45.917
and show it on two different
clusters at the same time.

00:24:45.917 --> 00:24:47.500
So the idea here is,
if you're running

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:49.330
in two different
availability zones

00:24:49.330 --> 00:24:51.010
or in two different
regions, you can

00:24:51.010 --> 00:24:56.020
deploy this app using one
set of edits into a get repo.

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:59.593
And then those will propagate
off to each of the clusters.

00:24:59.593 --> 00:25:01.510
And if you wanted to do
a progressive rollout,

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:04.360
you could insert a
cloud build pipeline,

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:07.228
which basically, each
cluster gets its config,

00:25:07.228 --> 00:25:09.520
and then 10 minutes or an
hour later, it could go ahead

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:10.853
and get the other one.

00:25:10.853 --> 00:25:13.270
And you could stick a canary
analysis service or something

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:14.500
else in between.

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:16.370
So, with that said,
let's go through

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:17.870
and kind of show
you the setup here.

00:25:17.870 --> 00:25:19.820
So we have two clusters.

00:25:19.820 --> 00:25:24.280
One is in US central one
and one is in US east four.

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:27.820
And these are just
really simple clusters.

00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:29.800
You'll see currently,
on the URL here,

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:31.270
that there's nothing there.

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:33.590
And on here, there's
nothing there.

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:36.005
And when we switch over--

00:25:36.005 --> 00:25:37.670
I'll go over here.

00:25:37.670 --> 00:25:42.460
So what we'll see here is
we have, in this window,

00:25:42.460 --> 00:25:45.610
is looking at all the
pods for cluster one.

00:25:45.610 --> 00:25:48.490
Here, we're looking at all
the pods for cluster two.

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:51.380
And here, we're looking at
cluster two's service roles,

00:25:51.380 --> 00:25:53.110
so these are policies.

00:25:53.110 --> 00:25:54.968
And this here is
my get repo, local

00:25:54.968 --> 00:25:57.010
get repo, where I'm going
to be making my changes

00:25:57.010 --> 00:25:58.390
and pushing them.

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:01.060
Now this gets sent up to GitHub.

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:02.890
And GitHub is right
here, which you

00:26:02.890 --> 00:26:08.020
can see also, if I refresh
it, has nothing in it.

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:09.670
It is just a blank
thing with system,

00:26:09.670 --> 00:26:11.230
which is the default way.

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:14.020
So, these clusters, I've
installed the Istio Operator

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:16.840
and I've installed the Anthos
Config Management Operator.

00:26:16.840 --> 00:26:22.090
And then I configured the
Anthos Config Operator as such.

00:26:22.090 --> 00:26:31.673
So what we can see here is,
when we look at cluster one,

00:26:31.673 --> 00:26:33.340
you'll see that it's
just a regular YAML

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:35.770
to configure Config Management.

00:26:35.770 --> 00:26:37.750
And we give it a cluster name.

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:39.730
So amazingly, in
Kubernetes, there

00:26:39.730 --> 00:26:41.770
is no cluster identity built in.

00:26:41.770 --> 00:26:43.510
So we actually give
each cluster a name,

00:26:43.510 --> 00:26:45.460
so we can refer
to it for purposes

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:48.610
of being able to target
policies at a given cluster.

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:50.500
We then tell it where to go.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:54.190
So I'm telling it, hey, go to
GitHub, and go to this root

00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:55.050
here.

00:26:55.050 --> 00:26:57.080
And then I could
tell it which branch.

00:26:57.080 --> 00:26:58.313
So I'm saying go to master.

00:26:58.313 --> 00:27:00.730
And if you wanted to go to
canary, you could go to canary.

00:27:00.730 --> 00:27:02.440
You could end up
creating a branch

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:04.120
and using the get
merge functionality

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:05.580
to push things along.

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:07.330
At this point, I'm
telling you to use SSH.

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:09.280
We support SSH and get cookies.

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:12.832
And I'm saying, hey, use this
root as the policy directory.

00:27:12.832 --> 00:27:14.290
And that means you
can use one repo

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:16.900
and have different environments
in one repo if you want,

00:27:16.900 --> 00:27:18.610
or you could have
different repos.

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:21.760
It's up to each
person or environment.

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:25.100
So anyways, I've installed
that on two clusters.

00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:26.500
And so let's go.

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:29.393
At this point, what
I'm going to do

00:27:29.393 --> 00:27:31.060
is I'm going to deploy
the Bookinfo app.

00:27:31.060 --> 00:27:32.643
But I'm going to
deploy it insecurely.

00:27:32.643 --> 00:27:33.850
So everything is plain text.

00:27:33.850 --> 00:27:34.990
There's no security rules.

00:27:34.990 --> 00:27:38.600
And everything can
talk to each other.

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:40.730
And so what you'll
see here is, I've

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:43.160
created a new
namespaces directory.

00:27:43.160 --> 00:27:46.310
I've created a
Bookinfo namespace.

00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:47.930
I've created the
namespace YAML, which

00:27:47.930 --> 00:27:50.480
is the same one you
would kubectl apply.

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:52.910
I've created a Bookinfo
which has a set of services

00:27:52.910 --> 00:27:56.390
and a set of deployments,
which map to the four

00:27:56.390 --> 00:27:58.640
different services
that are said,

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:00.890
and a Bookinfo gateway,
which exposes it

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:02.990
to the outside of my cluster.

00:28:02.990 --> 00:28:05.240
So at this point, I'm going
to go ahead and add this--

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:12.830
initial deployment and
then I'm going to get push.

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:19.870
So that gets pushed up.

00:28:19.870 --> 00:28:21.480
And then we can do--
what we'll notice

00:28:21.480 --> 00:28:25.150
on the right hand side is
these pods will start creating.

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:26.260
And there we go.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:28.200
So cluster two picked it up.

00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:30.180
And it's going ahead
and deploying the app

00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:34.770
as we review this.

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:36.633
And then we have
that on cluster one.

00:28:36.633 --> 00:28:38.300
So what's going on
in the background is,

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:40.670
there's an agent running
on each cluster that's

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:43.310
looking at that GitHub
repo I showed you earlier.

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:45.530
And it senses that there's
a new change in there.

00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:48.170
And it goes ahead and pulls down
that stuff and kubectl applies

00:28:48.170 --> 00:28:51.650
everything in that
repo automatically.

00:28:51.650 --> 00:28:55.880
OK so, when we go
into namespaces,

00:28:55.880 --> 00:29:01.460
you'll see here that these are
just everyday standard files.

00:29:01.460 --> 00:29:02.870
So here we have a service.

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:05.000
Here we have a deployment--

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:08.300
service, deployment,
ports, et cetera--

00:29:08.300 --> 00:29:09.950
OK, cool.

00:29:09.950 --> 00:29:12.950
So with that said,
what we should see here

00:29:12.950 --> 00:29:18.300
is our bookstore app.

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:21.130
And we have the details,
which is one service.

00:29:21.130 --> 00:29:24.210
And we have the product page,
which is this overall page.

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:25.860
And we have the
book reviews, which

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:27.610
is comprised of two services.

00:29:27.610 --> 00:29:30.240
One is the review and
one is the rating.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:33.010
And you'll notice that
in the Bookinfo example,

00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:37.170
there's three versions
of the ratings.

00:29:37.170 --> 00:29:38.700
And if I refresh
this, you'll see

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:41.400
that we are doing Round Robin.

00:29:41.400 --> 00:29:42.740
The network's not as fast here.

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:47.100
And these should change
over time, as in, this

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:48.630
is a different version.

00:29:48.630 --> 00:29:50.260
And they're changing.

00:29:50.260 --> 00:29:52.032
So this is showing us
that Istio routing.

00:29:52.032 --> 00:29:54.240
And what you could do with
Istio, which is also cool,

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:56.910
which Gobind didn't
cover, is there's also

00:29:56.910 --> 00:29:57.870
this idea of routing.

00:29:57.870 --> 00:30:00.120
And so you can run multiple
versions at the same time.

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:02.880
And you could do traffic
shaping and traffic spreading,

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:05.743
and roll out 5%, 10%, et
cetera, to new versions,

00:30:05.743 --> 00:30:08.160
to make sure that things are
happening, in addition to all

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:09.780
the security benefits.

00:30:09.780 --> 00:30:12.210
OK, so now that we have this
deployed, it's insecure.

00:30:12.210 --> 00:30:15.630
What we want to do is, we
want to layer encryption

00:30:15.630 --> 00:30:16.510
on top of this.

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:20.010
So, we want all the pieces here,
instead of being plain text,

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:22.410
we want them to actually
have the nice little barrel

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:23.700
of encryption around them.

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:26.590
So, let's figure
out how to do that.

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:28.000
So, I go back here.

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:31.980
And what you'll notice is,
in the current images--

00:30:31.980 --> 00:30:33.990
so, I've gotten all the
pods that are there.

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:35.573
are the images that
are running, which

00:30:35.573 --> 00:30:38.550
is just the Bookinfo software.

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:42.750
As we stated earlier, is
that Istio has a sidecar

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:44.910
proxy that runs as a container.

00:30:44.910 --> 00:30:47.640
And that gets injected
when you run your pods.

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:50.325
And that's where
the proxy happens.

00:30:50.325 --> 00:30:54.720
And the way that the Docker
networking stack gets set up

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:56.880
is, we only allow
those pods to talk

00:30:56.880 --> 00:30:58.650
to the proxy and nothing else.

00:30:58.650 --> 00:31:02.040
So now, all ingress and egress
must transit through the proxy.

00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:03.455
And that gives Istio--

00:31:03.455 --> 00:31:05.340
that gives you the
security guarantees

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:07.180
that Istio gives you.

00:31:07.180 --> 00:31:08.960
So let's do that.

00:31:08.960 --> 00:31:12.160
So enable MTLS on each.

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:14.660
You'll see here that I ended
up with some extra files, which

00:31:14.660 --> 00:31:17.990
I'll talk about
in a minute here.

00:31:17.990 --> 00:31:21.200
And let's go ahead
and add some--

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:25.800
so we're adding some MTLS.

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:27.060
We're pushing that up.

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:31.200
Let's go look what
actually happened here.

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:36.952
When we go to Bookinfo and
we look now at the namespace,

00:31:36.952 --> 00:31:38.660
you'll see here that
we have this comment

00:31:38.660 --> 00:31:41.600
called Istio injection enabled.

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:46.190
And I'll do this, because
syntax highlighting is fun.

00:31:46.190 --> 00:31:48.060
So we have Istio
injection enabled.

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:50.570
And what this says is,
anytime you create a pod,

00:31:50.570 --> 00:31:53.960
go ahead and automatically
add the Istio sidecar

00:31:53.960 --> 00:31:58.460
to that pod, the container,
the Istio proxy container

00:31:58.460 --> 00:32:00.980
to that pod, and wire
it up so that they only

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:02.570
can talk through the proxy.

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:03.650
Cool.

00:32:03.650 --> 00:32:05.832
Now, now that we
have that enabled--

00:32:05.832 --> 00:32:07.790
oh, and we'll talk about
destination rules too,

00:32:07.790 --> 00:32:08.373
which are fun.

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:13.450
So with destination rules,
what we're saying here

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:17.980
is, hey, if I'm going to go
ahead and talk to the product

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:20.560
page, I'm going to
use Istio mutual.

00:32:20.560 --> 00:32:23.170
And that means that when
I talk outbound to egress,

00:32:23.170 --> 00:32:25.350
I'm going to use--

00:32:25.350 --> 00:32:29.050
and I want to talk to the review
service, I'm going to use MTLS.

00:32:29.050 --> 00:32:30.610
And this is set
for all the things,

00:32:30.610 --> 00:32:33.400
ratings reviews, details.

00:32:33.400 --> 00:32:35.620
And this says, hey,
on those arrows,

00:32:35.620 --> 00:32:39.260
go ahead and encrypt those
with MTLS when I talk.

00:32:39.260 --> 00:32:41.120
And then the other
thing that we did here

00:32:41.120 --> 00:32:47.000
is, at the cluster level,
when I go to cluster,

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:48.640
I have a mesh policy now.

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:50.767
Now, within CSP
Config Management,

00:32:50.767 --> 00:32:52.850
when you have put things
in the cluster directory,

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:55.370
that gets pushed to the root
level of each cluster that's

00:32:55.370 --> 00:32:56.493
being managed.

00:32:56.493 --> 00:32:58.160
And so, now, cluster
one and cluster two

00:32:58.160 --> 00:33:01.190
both have this mesh policy
that says the following.

00:33:01.190 --> 00:33:03.760
It sets a default
policy and it says MTLS.

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:08.660
So it says any pods
in any namespace

00:33:08.660 --> 00:33:11.180
must use MTLS to be talked to.

00:33:11.180 --> 00:33:13.610
And so that helps you as
an administrator guarantee

00:33:13.610 --> 00:33:16.652
that you're using MTLS.

00:33:16.652 --> 00:33:18.110
Now what we're
going to notice here

00:33:18.110 --> 00:33:23.120
is, because we've changed
the makeup of the pods, that

00:33:23.120 --> 00:33:24.890
doesn't reconcile automatically.

00:33:24.890 --> 00:33:26.390
And when we show
the current images,

00:33:26.390 --> 00:33:28.970
we still have the
Istio Bookinfo.

00:33:28.970 --> 00:33:30.278
So we need to restart the pods.

00:33:30.278 --> 00:33:32.570
And so I'm going to show you
a feature of Anthos Config

00:33:32.570 --> 00:33:37.490
Management, which is called--
which my co-worker facetiously

00:33:37.490 --> 00:33:39.950
named Insistent Reconciliation.

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:42.530
And that means that, whatever
the state that's in the repo,

00:33:42.530 --> 00:33:45.390
we guarantee that we make
the cluster that state.

00:33:45.390 --> 00:33:47.450
So, if I destroy the
deployments, which

00:33:47.450 --> 00:33:50.240
I defined in the repo,
the Anthos Config Manager

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:53.040
will automatically
recreate them.

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:55.460
So I'm going to go ahead
and kill those deployments.

00:33:55.460 --> 00:33:58.250
And you'll see here that, here
they've gotten all deleted.

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:00.350
You'll see that you'll
have a bunch terminating.

00:34:00.350 --> 00:34:03.300
And now you'll have a bunch
initializing at the same time.

00:34:03.300 --> 00:34:06.080
And so that happened because
the Anthos Config Manager

00:34:06.080 --> 00:34:08.460
wants the cluster to be
in the state of the repo

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:10.697
so we can have that guarantee.

00:34:10.697 --> 00:34:12.280
So at this point,
what I'm going to do

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:14.850
is show the current images.

00:34:14.850 --> 00:34:17.199
And what you're going to
see here is, three of them

00:34:17.199 --> 00:34:19.298
have now picked up
the Istio proxy.

00:34:19.298 --> 00:34:21.340
And then you have the
three that are terminating,

00:34:21.340 --> 00:34:23.150
which will go away soon.

00:34:23.150 --> 00:34:25.989
So now we have
Istio proxy sitting

00:34:25.989 --> 00:34:29.830
in front of our existing pods.

00:34:29.830 --> 00:34:32.590
We have told the
system to use MTLS.

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:35.422
We have encryption
across all setups.

00:34:35.422 --> 00:34:36.880
So at this point,
what I should see

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:41.080
is, when I refresh this,
assuming everything is speedy,

00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:43.480
is the Book app
should be working,

00:34:43.480 --> 00:34:46.870
as is, which it looks
like it is-- sweet.

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:49.510
Except now, everything
is talking encrypted,

00:34:49.510 --> 00:34:51.620
between all these
different points--

00:34:51.620 --> 00:34:52.690
OK, cool.

00:34:52.690 --> 00:34:54.650
So next step, now
they've got encryption,

00:34:54.650 --> 00:34:58.342
what we want is the ability
to lock things down.

00:34:58.342 --> 00:34:59.800
And we want to say,
you know what--

00:34:59.800 --> 00:35:01.300
it's great that you
have encryption,

00:35:01.300 --> 00:35:03.060
it's great that you
have authentication,

00:35:03.060 --> 00:35:05.960
but now let's put some
authorization into the mix.

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:09.490
And we're going to start by
just turning on authorization,

00:35:09.490 --> 00:35:13.160
but setting no policy,
so it's deny by default.

00:35:13.160 --> 00:35:17.150
So, we're going to go ahead
and enable service Rbac.

00:35:17.150 --> 00:35:19.290
End you'll see here that
we've got a cluster Rbac.

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:20.870
So let's go ahead and push that.

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:31.070
Get commit dash m, add
Rbac, and then get push.

00:35:31.070 --> 00:35:33.400
So, when that goes
ahead and push,

00:35:33.400 --> 00:35:35.165
let's go look at that
cluster Rbac policy

00:35:35.165 --> 00:35:36.040
and see what it says.

00:35:40.420 --> 00:35:41.470
So here we go.

00:35:41.470 --> 00:35:43.270
We have a cluster Rbac config.

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:47.070
We have applied at
the named default,

00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:49.600
so it's the root one for
all the whole cluster.

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:54.440
And it's saying, for this
namespace, please enforce.

00:35:54.440 --> 00:35:56.100
And enforce is deny
by default, which

00:35:56.100 --> 00:35:59.430
means you have to explicitly
list the Rbac service

00:35:59.430 --> 00:36:02.190
binding in order for
things to talk to services.

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:07.170
And so now, this forces all
of the application writers

00:36:07.170 --> 00:36:13.270
on your clusters to explicitly
detail their dependencies.

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:14.878
So in this case,
we're going to want

00:36:14.878 --> 00:36:16.920
to be able to-- each one
of these things is going

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:19.253
to require rules, so product
page could talk to reviews,

00:36:19.253 --> 00:36:21.090
and product page
could talk to details,

00:36:21.090 --> 00:36:24.090
and reviews can talk to ratings.

00:36:24.090 --> 00:36:24.990
So we push that.

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:26.473
We're saying it's enforced.

00:36:26.473 --> 00:36:28.890
What I would expect to happen
here, now that we have that,

00:36:28.890 --> 00:36:30.990
is the app should break.

00:36:30.990 --> 00:36:32.540
And it has.

00:36:32.540 --> 00:36:34.290
And you'll see here
that on both clusters,

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:36.060
I get Rbac access denied.

00:36:36.060 --> 00:36:38.050
I tried to hit the product page.

00:36:38.050 --> 00:36:40.893
When I hit there there,
it said there's Rbac.

00:36:40.893 --> 00:36:42.810
There's no Rbac authorization
allowing my user

00:36:42.810 --> 00:36:45.330
to get the product page,
so it said go away--

00:36:45.330 --> 00:36:46.170
great.

00:36:46.170 --> 00:36:49.230
So with that said, let's go
ahead and add a partial rule

00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:51.130
to show how you can
add authorization

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:52.390
on a piecemeal basis.

00:36:59.190 --> 00:37:00.690
So we're going to do--

00:37:03.820 --> 00:37:05.400
adding an Rbac policy.

00:37:05.400 --> 00:37:08.107
So you'll see here that we
got a namespace policy file.

00:37:08.107 --> 00:37:09.690
We're going to go
ahead and add that--

00:37:13.750 --> 00:37:17.260
partial policy, and get push.

00:37:17.260 --> 00:37:18.690
So while that's going on--

00:37:18.690 --> 00:37:20.560
and again, all this is
happening through Anthos Config

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:21.060
Management.

00:37:21.060 --> 00:37:22.540
I'm not running kubectl at all.

00:37:22.540 --> 00:37:24.460
It's all happening
behind the scenes.

00:37:24.460 --> 00:37:27.980
When I go into the namespace,
there's extra credit

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:30.530
if people can realize
a couple of things

00:37:30.530 --> 00:37:33.970
here about the security
aspects of these policies

00:37:33.970 --> 00:37:34.700
end of the page.

00:37:34.700 --> 00:37:38.450
I'll see if anyone
identified them at the end

00:37:38.450 --> 00:37:39.480
and go from there.

00:37:39.480 --> 00:37:42.570
So when we look at
this namespace policy,

00:37:42.570 --> 00:37:44.940
you'll see here I have
a service viewer role.

00:37:44.940 --> 00:37:48.250
And what I'm saying is,
is that all services--

00:37:48.250 --> 00:37:54.480
so basically matching any
URL, any method, get--

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:59.100
and I'm saying
allow these apps--

00:37:59.100 --> 00:38:01.980
product page in details
to talk to each other.

00:38:01.980 --> 00:38:04.020
So I'm allowing the
product page to talk,

00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:07.470
and I'm allowing people to
talk to the product page.

00:38:07.470 --> 00:38:09.960
But the details are
being able to be

00:38:09.960 --> 00:38:12.390
talked to by the product page.

00:38:12.390 --> 00:38:15.990
But notice what's missing
here-- ratings and reviews.

00:38:15.990 --> 00:38:17.740
And then down here, I
have a role binding,

00:38:17.740 --> 00:38:20.110
which binds both the
Istio system namespace.

00:38:20.110 --> 00:38:22.230
So it can get telemetry.

00:38:22.230 --> 00:38:24.700
And the Bookinfo
namespace, which says,

00:38:24.700 --> 00:38:26.850
hey, things running a
Bookinfo are allowed

00:38:26.850 --> 00:38:29.460
to be given this role,
as long as they have

00:38:29.460 --> 00:38:31.650
the constraints of the
destination labels,

00:38:31.650 --> 00:38:35.730
being product and details.

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:40.170
So what we should notice now
is a partially working app

00:38:40.170 --> 00:38:41.070
on both clusters.

00:38:41.070 --> 00:38:42.397
And that's what we have.

00:38:42.397 --> 00:38:43.230
We have the details.

00:38:43.230 --> 00:38:44.550
We have the product page.

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:48.030
But we have no product reviews.

00:38:48.030 --> 00:38:50.730
There's been an error, because
it's got in Rbac denied,

00:38:50.730 --> 00:38:53.330
which will return as a 403.

00:38:53.330 --> 00:38:56.220
OK, cool, great, we've been
able to partially do that.

00:38:56.220 --> 00:38:57.678
Let's add the full--

00:38:57.678 --> 00:38:59.970
and by the way, just to show
you that this is all real,

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:05.910
we have all these things
within the GitHub repo.

00:39:05.910 --> 00:39:08.620
OK, so we're going to go
ahead and add the full service

00:39:08.620 --> 00:39:11.200
Rbac policy.

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:15.690
And from there, we're getting--

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:22.792
OK, so that's pushing.

00:39:22.792 --> 00:39:25.000
You notice, out here, we
have a service viewer policy

00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:26.950
that just got pushed earlier.

00:39:26.950 --> 00:39:30.940
With that said, let's look
at our namespace policy now.

00:39:30.940 --> 00:39:33.160
You'll see that
we've added reviews

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:35.410
and we've added ratings.

00:39:35.410 --> 00:39:37.900
So at this point, everything
is allowed to talk.

00:39:37.900 --> 00:39:39.790
We have enabled that.

00:39:39.790 --> 00:39:41.920
And so let's write that out--

00:39:41.920 --> 00:39:42.850
cool.

00:39:42.850 --> 00:39:44.290
That's been pushed earlier.

00:39:44.290 --> 00:39:46.480
We should notice that
the app starts working.

00:39:46.480 --> 00:39:49.340
And there it is.

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:52.730
And doing the right
thing through that.

00:39:52.730 --> 00:39:55.160
So great, we've just
now gone back through.

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:58.300
We've started with
an empty cluster,

00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:01.990
just with Istio and just with
Anthos Config Management.

00:40:01.990 --> 00:40:05.600
We deployed the
Bookinfo app insecurely.

00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:07.510
We layered on MTLS.

00:40:07.510 --> 00:40:12.667
We added cluster Rbac policy
saying deny by default.

00:40:12.667 --> 00:40:14.500
We layered on a couple
of different policies

00:40:14.500 --> 00:40:16.760
to have the app be able
to talk to each other.

00:40:16.760 --> 00:40:20.410
And at this point, we have
a fully kind of enabled Rbac

00:40:20.410 --> 00:40:23.350
Istio application, that has
been working from the beginning,

00:40:23.350 --> 00:40:26.140
from the insecure state, to
adding and layering on Istio.

00:40:26.140 --> 00:40:29.800
And I never changed a line of
code in the Istio Bookinfo app.

00:40:29.800 --> 00:40:32.950
And to finish it off, I'm
going to go ahead and remove

00:40:32.950 --> 00:40:33.880
everything.

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:35.668
So that takes my
repo back to normal.

00:40:35.668 --> 00:40:37.210
And then I'm just
going to push this.

00:40:37.210 --> 00:40:46.410
So get add dash a,
get commit, dash m.

00:40:46.410 --> 00:40:50.910
Kill all, and then I'm
going to push that.

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:58.410
And, as a final thing,
if I actually go up here,

00:40:58.410 --> 00:41:01.152
what we will see is all of these
things will go to terminating.

00:41:01.152 --> 00:41:03.360
They will remove the clusters,
because if they're not

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:04.950
in Anthos Config
Management, we go ahead

00:41:04.950 --> 00:41:06.280
and we'll take them
out of the clusters.

00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:07.470
That's one of the things
that we guarantee.

00:41:07.470 --> 00:41:09.480
Again, we want the
state of the clusters

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:11.610
to match the state of the repo.

00:41:11.610 --> 00:41:15.300
And so, with that, that's kind
of our demo of Anthos Config

00:41:15.300 --> 00:41:17.940
Management and of Istio.

00:41:17.940 --> 00:41:18.940
Thank you, everyone.

00:41:18.940 --> 00:41:22.370
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:23.962 --> 00:41:25.670
Can we switch back to
the slides, please.

00:41:29.780 --> 00:41:30.410
Thanks, Ray.

00:41:30.410 --> 00:41:32.210
Thanks for that awesome demo.

00:41:32.210 --> 00:41:35.883
So, originally I had said that
your workflow might change.

00:41:35.883 --> 00:41:37.550
That's actually what
I was referring to.

00:41:37.550 --> 00:41:39.133
Instead of using
kubectl, you'll start

00:41:39.133 --> 00:41:40.310
doing get push everywhere.

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:41.660
It's going to be awesome.

00:41:41.660 --> 00:41:43.400
So thank you for that.

00:41:43.400 --> 00:41:46.580
So we've put all
of this together.

00:41:46.580 --> 00:41:49.300
We actually looked at this
diagram here, if you go back.

00:41:49.300 --> 00:41:53.120
So Ray helped us see some of
the core pieces of this picture,

00:41:53.120 --> 00:41:56.340
just from the power of
get push, which is great.

00:41:56.340 --> 00:41:58.640
You can do this across
all your clusters.

00:41:58.640 --> 00:42:01.240
It'll give you a lot of peace
of mind, because you'll be sure

00:42:01.240 --> 00:42:02.990
that all your clusters
are running exactly

00:42:02.990 --> 00:42:05.570
the posture that you want.

00:42:05.570 --> 00:42:09.040
So going forward,
we've talked about how

00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:12.510
to deploy these policies, how
to construct these policies,

00:42:12.510 --> 00:42:15.420
but there's one more piece of
the puzzle, which is, how do

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:16.670
you keep an eye on everything?

00:42:16.670 --> 00:42:19.690
And that's where GCP
Console and Anthos really

00:42:19.690 --> 00:42:22.390
brings it all together
for you as an ecosystem.

00:42:22.390 --> 00:42:27.130
You can connect all
your GKE clusters

00:42:27.130 --> 00:42:30.610
or Anthos clusters
to GCP Console

00:42:30.610 --> 00:42:32.722
and you can see them
all in one place.

00:42:32.722 --> 00:42:34.180
You can see all
the details related

00:42:34.180 --> 00:42:36.640
to that, any of your clusters,
wherever they're running.

00:42:36.640 --> 00:42:39.990
Again, it could be on-prem, it
could be GCP, it could be AWS.

00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:42.040
All you have to do is
log into those clusters

00:42:42.040 --> 00:42:44.200
from GCP's console and
you'll be able to see

00:42:44.200 --> 00:42:47.770
all the relevant details
of the control plane

00:42:47.770 --> 00:42:50.050
in your GCP console.

00:42:50.050 --> 00:42:51.630
As well, that goes
for stack driver.

00:42:51.630 --> 00:42:54.220
Stack driver is
are logging tool.

00:42:54.220 --> 00:42:56.830
You can use stack driver
to pull all the logs

00:42:56.830 --> 00:42:58.690
from your different
clusters that you're

00:42:58.690 --> 00:43:02.560
running across the planet and
bring them to stack driver

00:43:02.560 --> 00:43:03.340
for analysis.

00:43:03.340 --> 00:43:06.620
You could push them through
to BigQuery if you wanted to.

00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:07.990
You can also use other tools.

00:43:07.990 --> 00:43:09.080
If you're familiar with--

00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:13.570
if you prefer Prometheus and
Grafana for graphing them,

00:43:13.570 --> 00:43:14.680
that's also great.

00:43:14.680 --> 00:43:17.195
And if you wanted to use
Splunk or Elastic, all of that

00:43:17.195 --> 00:43:18.070
is supported as well.

00:43:21.450 --> 00:43:23.112
We're close to the finish line.

00:43:23.112 --> 00:43:25.320
So really, the three things
that we started with here

00:43:25.320 --> 00:43:28.890
were, one, we wanted to give you
a defense in-depth experience

00:43:28.890 --> 00:43:32.880
using this fly by wire concept
of policy based security.

00:43:32.880 --> 00:43:35.250
That's really what
Anthos is all about.

00:43:35.250 --> 00:43:38.430
We talked in depth about
very high resolution security

00:43:38.430 --> 00:43:43.060
that you can get using Istio
and Kubernetes policies.

00:43:43.060 --> 00:43:47.130
And finally, we looked at Anthos
Config Management as a way

00:43:47.130 --> 00:43:51.120
to do git ops and put your
infrastructure as code, really

00:43:51.120 --> 00:43:53.500
as a workflow for
CICD pipelines.

00:43:53.500 --> 00:43:56.160
So you can actually take
all of your security posture

00:43:56.160 --> 00:43:57.930
and replicate it
across as many clusters

00:43:57.930 --> 00:43:59.400
as you'd like in an instant.

00:44:02.420 --> 00:44:04.930
One final call outs to
all the other security

00:44:04.930 --> 00:44:06.550
things that you
have to think about

00:44:06.550 --> 00:44:08.530
as a network administrator.

00:44:08.530 --> 00:44:14.860
We've been talking about Anthos
clusters in very high detail.

00:44:14.860 --> 00:44:16.652
But outside the
cluster, there's a lot

00:44:16.652 --> 00:44:17.860
of other stuff happening too.

00:44:17.860 --> 00:44:19.235
So you have to
think about things

00:44:19.235 --> 00:44:20.998
like your other
infrastructure pieces.

00:44:20.998 --> 00:44:22.540
You have to think
about DDOS attacks.

00:44:22.540 --> 00:44:24.082
You have to think
about exfiltration.

00:44:24.082 --> 00:44:27.380
You have to think about
secure access to your nodes.

00:44:27.380 --> 00:44:30.580
So I highly encourage you to
look at all the other tools

00:44:30.580 --> 00:44:32.770
that GCP provides
for you to ensure

00:44:32.770 --> 00:44:38.277
that you have a security posture
that is comfortable for you.

00:44:38.277 --> 00:44:39.610
There are some related sessions.

00:44:39.610 --> 00:44:41.652
I know this is towards
the end of the conference.

00:44:41.652 --> 00:44:44.450
But they'll all be
available on YouTube.

00:44:44.450 --> 00:44:45.640
So please check them out.

00:44:45.640 --> 00:44:48.530
They're highly related to the
content that we've covered.

00:44:48.530 --> 00:44:53.382
And the rest of them are offered
here, below, at that link.

00:44:53.382 --> 00:44:55.340
With that, I'd like to
thank you for your time.

00:44:55.340 --> 00:44:58.722
I appreciate you guys staying
back on a Thursday afternoon.

00:44:58.722 --> 00:45:01.180
Thank you for coming here and
thank you for attending Next.

